timately connected with  them  and i  feel the same way with the languages  that i've learned  maybe it's because i do a lot of  listening so  you know i'm not deliberately trying to  learn anything i do when i'm you know  after listening i i review it on my ipad  and look up words and stuff but i'm not  deliberately trying to learn anything  i'm more trying to  become friends with the language and try  to  start to get in the mood  to enjoy the language and i think  that helps in terms of  pronunciation for example in terms of  intonation in terms of using the right  phrasing  as we start to become familiar with the  language even if we don't fully  understand it a lot of these things are  kind of  penetrating us i noticed this in my  arabic and my and my persian  uh now  paradoxically and and remember again  that many of you are studying languages  at school or you may be studying them  for professional reasons which was my  case when i was learning chinese 50  years ago that was my job i was doing it  seven hours a day but now i'm doing it  as a hobby  i'm doing it out of interest  i'm doing it because i'm interested not  only in the language but in the history  and the culture behind that language and  possibly travel and food and meeting  people and that whole  sense of being able to participate in  another  cultural media  so that's what motivates me  and what i found recently is that  my strategy of  you know and this is this whole issue of  studying more than one language say two  languages at the same time  you know what's the best way to combine  languages should we be doing two  languages the same day or alternating  days  and i'm beginning to find that if i go  one week on one and one week on the  other  then when i come back to the first one  i'm sort of reconnecting with an old  friend  and that's a nice feeling and i'm even  finding and as i said before this  business of benign neglect if you leave  one language go to the other one for a  while come back to the first one all of  a sudden things are clearer  maybe because you've pushed your brain  to work this other language  and maybe because if we stay with the  one language and we're trying to learn  it and we're disappointed that we're not  doing better in a certain amount of  fresh frustration  creeps in whereas if we leave it for a  while do this other language and then we  come back to the first one we rediscover  an old friend everything is fresh again  and  i do find that i hear things more  clearly  i'm a little more aware of of of the  language  and  getting to where we are familiar with  the language with the intonation with  the pronunciation with the word choices  this is all part of learning the  language it's all part of our brains  getting used to the language  and  if we can develop this sort of natural  getting to know the language we're going  to speak more naturally as opposed to  trying to learn it through rules and  and tests and and things of that nature  and it's surprising i i was uh spent a  while with with uh persian then i  started listening to my uh arabic uh  podcasts again and  and and i find that you know i i  recognize my old friend i recognize some  of the personality of the arab speaker  he's sort of very intent on getting his  view across and he uses certain  expressions  in the uh certainly in the uh in the  standard arabic which i'm listening to  from you know these political podcasts  uh the  egyptian arabic has a different  personality and i have a very good tutor  who's creating content for me in  egyptian arabic and so i can get  familiar with and and get to know and  get to feel the personality of egyptian  arabic  and then i'll leave that and then i'll  go to  my persian again and on each of these  occasions  it's staying fresh for me and then i'm  rediscovering an old friend picking up  where we left off  and i find that i'm  progressing perhaps or enjoying it more  or less frustrated by my lack of  progress and more 